lsrecent (1.16.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * A settings menu: `lsrecent config` on a terminal, or `c` / `/config` at any
    listing prompt. Every setting numbered with its value and origin; type a
    number to change it (on/off settings toggle, sort picks from a list, the
    rest are typed and checked), d<number> resets one, e opens the file. Saved
    at once. From a listing, the listing redraws with the new settings.
  * `lsrecent config show` is the old non-interactive listing, and what bare
    `config` still prints when piped.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 15:30:00 +0200

lsrecent (1.15.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * Busy-day focus. When TODAY holds at least `focus_today` repos (default 10),
    the plain listing shows today and nothing else, and enter at the prompt
    prints the next page of today instead of staying put; `q` stays. Once today
    runs out, enter stays as before. The older repos are still numbered after
    today's, `n` pages on to them and `lsrepos --all` prints everything. Never
    applies to a search, a timeframe, --today/--yesterday or --all.
    `lsrecent config set focus_today 0` turns it off.
  * Repo names print with hyphens and underscores as spaces ("Wheres My Stuff"),
    `pretty_names` (default 1). Display only. Search folds hyphen, underscore
    and space together, so "wheres my" and "wheres-my" both find it.
  * Timeframes: `--since today|yesterday|week|month|3d|2w|1m` on the command
    line (`lsrepos --since 3d claude`), and `@today claude`, `@3d`, `@2w x` at
    the prompt; `@` alone drops the timeframe, `/` clears both. Calendar days,
    today included, cut over the listing's own dates rather than the visit log
    alone, so `@today` keeps exactly what is under the TODAY header.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 14:30:00 +0200

lsrecent (1.14.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * A settings file, ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/lsrecent/config, holding the
    six tunables that until now existed only as LSRECENT_* variables: sort,
    prompt, max_rows, noise_pct, repo_root, repo_depth. `key = value`, one per
    line, `#` comments, and meant to be hand-edited as much as written to.
  * `ss` at the prompt keeps the order in force. Which order you want is a
    question you answer by pressing `s` until the screen looks right, and being
    told at that moment to quit and run a second command from memory is how a
    preference stays unset for a year.
  * `lsrecent config` reports every setting, its value, and whether that value is
    coming from the file, from a variable in this shell, or from the built-in
    default. `config set|unset|get|edit|path` for the rest.
  * A flag beats the environment, the environment beats the file, the file beats
    the default — which falls out of the loader filling in only the LSRECENT_*
    variables that are not already set, rather than being enforced anywhere. A
    setting written while the matching variable is exported says so, instead of
    reporting a success that will not be visible.
  * A line the settings file gets wrong is ignored and reported on every run
    rather than taken as read: the file is parsed by every bare `ls` in an
    override directory, so one stale typo must not take the shell prompt with it,
    and falling back to the default in silence is the failure that looks like
    success. `check` and `visit` stay quiet — they run on every prompt and every
    directory change, and read no settings.
  * Parsed in-process rather than sourced. Sourcing would have made the config
    file a shell script, in which a typo is an arbitrary command.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 01:05:00 +0300

lsrecent (1.13.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * A positional argument that is not written like a path is now a search rather
    than an error: `lsrepos wms`, `lsr wms`, `lstoday claude`. The set is reduced
    before bucketing, so it prints as an ordinary grouped listing containing only
    matches, numbered from 1 and answered by the same prompt. An argument with a
    slash in it, or `.` / `..`, is still the root — deciding by `-d` instead would
    have made `lsrepos smart-home` mean different things depending on where you
    were standing.
  * A search that matches exactly one repository cds there instead of asking. At
    the prompt, enter takes the only match of a search; enter otherwise still
    means stay put, and `q` and ctrl-d always do.
  * Prompt searches are ranked in four tiers — whole name, starts-with, contains,
    path-only — and the matched characters are highlighted inside the name. Before
    this, `wms` could return `Wheres-My-Stuff` fourth, under three repos whose
    paths happened to contain the letters, with nothing on screen saying why any
    row was there.
  * A fourth order: `--frecency` (`-f`), `sf` at the prompt. Visits first, most
    recent breaking the ties. It differs from `--visits` exactly at the ties, and
    the ties are most of a bucket: `--visits` sends them to the alphabet, this
    leaves them in recency order.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 00:20:00 +0300

lsrecent (1.12.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * The default order inside a bucket is most-recent-first again, not
    alphabetical. Alphabetical (1.10.0) was argued from the coarse buckets, where
    everything is the same age and ranking it moves names around for no visible
    reason. TODAY is not that bucket: it is read top-down, and the last repository
    you opened is nearly always the one you are going back to, so it belongs on
    line 1 rather than wherever its initial falls. `sn` at the prompt restores the
    alphabet in one keystroke.
  * The three orders can be selected directly at the prompt as well as cycled:
    `sr` recency, `sv` visits, `sn` name. Cycling is fine when one of three is
    wanted; it is a guessing game when the one you want is two presses away and
    you have to read the screen between them. `s` still cycles, now recency →
    visits → name.
  * `--recency` and `--visits` are the preferred spellings of `--time` and
    `--activity`, and LSRECENT_SORT takes recency|visits|name. The old spellings
    still work everywhere.
  * Keys and prose no longer print in the same colour. Every hint the tool emits
    is part instruction and part explanation — `s sort`, `n for the next 20` —
    and in one orange the two fuse into a sentence you must read to find the one
    character you are meant to press. The key is now bright cyan, the prose stays
    orange, the setting a key would change is bright green, and separators are
    grey: the prompt line, `?`, the paging footer, the truncation footer and the
    noise footer all follow it. The `?` help also marks the order in force on the
    row that selects it, instead of naming it elsewhere on the line.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 22:30:00 +0300

lsrecent (1.11.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * A repository's date no longer comes from the mtime of its `.git` directory,
    which meant almost nothing. A directory's mtime moves when an entry inside it
    is created or removed, so every lock file, every `gc.log` and every temp file
    any tool writes next to the index counted as work — and one command run across
    the tree made all of them today's. Measured on 2026-08-08, a single sweep had
    moved the `.git` mtime of 300 of 303 repos while the worktree mtime had moved
    for 14; TODAY held three hundred repositories, which is the same as holding
    none. The signals are now the worktree mtime and `.git/logs/HEAD` — a commit,
    checkout, merge, pull or reset — plus the visit log as before. `.git/index` is
    excluded because `git status` rewrites it, and `.git/FETCH_HEAD` because a
    fetch is not work and a tree-wide fetch is exactly the sweep being guarded
    against; a pull moves HEAD and is caught by the reflog anyway.
  * A calendar day whose inferred dates cover more than half of all repos is now
    treated as a bulk touch: those dates are discarded, the repos land in a new
    UNDATED bucket at the bottom rather than in TODAY, and a footer says so and
    how to undo it. The threshold is $LSRECENT_NOISE_PCT (0 disables the rule).
    Visit-log dates are never discarded, which is what makes this safe — a sweep
    opens nothing, so a genuinely busy day is not at risk, and a repo caught in
    the sweep that you did open is dated by the visit and stays in TODAY.
  * `lsrecent noise` says what is being suppressed and why; `noise add [day]`
    names one the rule missed, `noise remove <day>` one it caught wrongly. Remove
    records the day as trusted rather than deleting it, since an auto-detected day
    would otherwise be re-detected on the next listing. The list lives in
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lsrecent/noisy-days.
  * A third order inside a bucket: `--activity` (`-A`) ranks by how many times the
    visit log recorded the repo being opened inside that bucket's own day, so the
    day you dipped into forty repositories and worked in four shows the four
    first. Ties fall to the name, so a bucket nobody visited looks exactly as it
    does under `--name`. LSRECENT_SORT now takes name, activity or time.
  * `s` at the prompt cycles the order — name, activity, time — without leaving
    the listing. It re-prints rather than re-ordering in place, and says
    "renumbered", because a number is a position in a ranking and changing the
    order makes a new one.
  * One `sort` for the whole listing instead of one per bucket, which is nine
    forks on a normal listing. This runs on every bare `ls` in a repo-grouping
    directory, and now on every press of `s` as well.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 20:15:00 +0300

lsrecent (1.10.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * `p` is now a real previous page rather than a jump back to the top, and `n`
    joins `+` as next. `t` is the way back to the top. Paging only in one
    direction was a defect dressed as a decision: the line telling you there are
    229 more repositories is exactly the line you overshoot, and scrolling the
    terminal to find the page again does not restore the prompt's idea of where
    you are.
  * Page length is now measured once per view instead of once per page, over the
    whole view rather than the slice about to be printed. Sized off the visible
    slice, a page's length depended on the longest label still ahead of it, so
    boundaries moved with your starting point and `p` could not land back on the
    page you had just left. Columns now also line up from one page to the next.
  * The footer, prompt, help and messages are orange (256-colour where the
    terminal has it, yellow where it does not) instead of bright black. Grey is
    the ink a theme reserves for what you are meant to skim past, which is the
    wrong instruction for the one line saying how to reach what did not fit.
    Entry numbers stay grey — there de-emphasis is the point.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0300

lsrecent (1.9.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * The last week is now a bucket per calendar day: TODAY, YESTERDAY, then
    2 DAYS AGO through 7 DAYS AGO. PAST WEEK is gone; PAST MONTH, PAST 3 MONTHS
    and OLDER are unchanged. "Today or yesterday?" was always the question these
    listings answer and it does not stop being the question at 48 hours — a
    single week-long bucket was routinely the biggest heap on the screen.
  * Empty days are not printed, so a quiet week costs nothing. A busy one is a
    longer listing; the screenful budget cuts it as before and everything below
    the fold is still numbered and reachable from the prompt.
  * The eight day boundaries are walked one snapped midnight at a time rather
    than multiplied out from today's, so a week containing a DST change still
    has seven days in it. Verified against all 365 days of 2026 in eight zones.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 11:46:46 +0300

lsrecent (1.8.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * Repos are now listed alphabetically within each recency bucket. The buckets
    still run newest-first and still carry the recency; ordering their members
    against each other ranked by a distinction the timestamps cannot really
    support, and moved a name around between listings for reasons invisible on
    screen. A stable position is what makes a bucket scannable for a repo you
    already have in mind.
  * `--time` (`-t`) sorts within a bucket newest-first as before, `--name`
    (`-n`) is the default, and LSRECENT_SORT=time makes --time the default.
  * Consequence: a listing cut to one screenful now cuts the bucket at the fold
    alphabetically rather than by age. Whole buckets are still dropped
    oldest-first, and everything cut is still numbered and reachable from the
    prompt.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 16:20:10 +0300

lsrecent (1.7.1) unstable; urgency=low

  * shell-init: unalias the names it defines as functions. An alias left over
    from 1.1.1 or earlier — lsrepos, lstoday — is expanded before the parser
    reaches `lsrepos()`, so re-sourcing in a long-lived shell died with
    "syntax error near unexpected token `('" and eval abandoned everything
    after it. Half the grouped views silently never got defined.

 -- Daniel Rosehill <daniel@danielrosehill.co.il>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 15:58:55 +0300

lsrecent (1.7.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * The cd prompt is now a browser: search by name or path, page with +, show
    everything with a, ? for help. A bare word searches; digits still select.
  * Numbers remain ranking positions under filtering and paging, so a number
    means the same repository everywhere and in `lsrecent path N` later.
  * Reaches the entries below the fold, which previously required abandoning
    the listing for `lsrepos --all` or the fzf picker.

